/* data/learn-c/phy-12-12.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-12.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-12"] = {"12.1":{"s":"Moving charges make magnetic fields. Faraday asked the reverse question: can a magnetic field make a current? Yes, if the field through a circuit changes. This is electromagnetic induction, the idea behind generators, transformers and induction cooktops.","w":["An emf is induced only when the flux changes. A steady field through a loop gives nothing.","Lenz's law gives the direction: the induced current opposes the change in flux."],"x":["Oersted showed in 1820 that a current makes a magnetic field. It was natural to ask the opposite question: can a magnetic field make a current? In 1831 Michael Faraday in England and Joseph Henry in the USA found that it can, but only when the magnetic field changes. A steady field produces nothing.","This effect, electromagnetic induction, is the basis of nearly all electric power: the generators in power stations, the transformers on the poles, the pickup in an electric guitar, wireless charging of phones, and induction cooktops.","In this chapter we study Faraday's laws and Lenz's law, motional emf, generators and motors, eddy currents, self- and mutual inductance, the energy stored in a magnetic field, and the transformer."],"e":"Moving a magnet in and out of a coil connected to a sensitive meter makes the needle swing one way as the magnet goes in and the other way as it comes out. When the magnet is still, the needle stays at zero."},"12.2":{"s":"Faraday's first law: whenever the magnetic flux linked with a circuit changes, an emf is induced in it. If the circuit is closed, a current flows. Second law: the size of the induced emf equals the rate of change of flux, e = −dΦ/dt.","f":[{"x":"e = -d(phi)/dt","n":"Faraday's law with Lenz's sign; phi = flux linked with the circuit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.2, p.266"},{"x":"e = -N*d(phi)/dt","n":"Coil of N turns; phi = flux through one turn.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.2, p.266"}],"w":["Only a CHANGE in flux induces an emf; a large steady flux gives none."],"x":["Magnetic flux through a surface measures the amount of magnetic field passing through it: Φ = B · A = BA cosθ, where θ is the angle between the field and the normal to the surface. Its unit is the weber (Wb) = tesla metre².","Faraday's laws: (1) Whenever the magnetic flux through a circuit changes, an emf is induced in it, and lasts only as long as the flux is changing. (2) The size of the induced emf is proportional to the rate of change of flux: ε = −N dΦ/dt, for a coil of N turns. The minus sign is the content of Lenz's law.","The flux can change in three ways: the field B can change in size (a changing current in a nearby coil, or a moving magnet), the area A can change (a coil being stretched or a sliding rod), or the angle θ can change (a coil rotating). The induced current in a closed circuit is I = ε/R, and the charge that flows is q = NΔΦ/R, which depends only on the total change in flux and not on the time taken.","A larger emf is produced by a faster change, more turns, or a stronger field."],"e":"The flux through a 100-turn coil falls from 0.05 Wb to 0.01 Wb in 0.2 s. The induced emf is 100 × 0.04/0.2 = 20 V."},"12.3":{"s":"Lenz's law gives the direction: the induced current always opposes the change that caused it. Push a magnet's north pole into a coil and the near face of the coil becomes a north pole, pushing back. This is conservation of energy: you must do work to produce the current.","f":[{"x":"The induced current opposes the change that causes it","n":"Lenz's law. Consequence of conservation of energy."}],"w":["The induced current opposes the change in flux, not the flux itself: with flux falling, it tries to keep it up.","If Lenz's law were the other way round, the current would grow by itself and break energy conservation."],"x":["Lenz's law gives the direction of the induced current: it flows in such a direction that its magnetic effect opposes the change in flux that causes it. If the flux is increasing, the induced current makes a field against it. If the flux is decreasing, the induced current makes a field in the same direction, trying to keep it.","This is the law of conservation of energy in disguise. If the induced current helped the change, then pushing a magnet towards a coil would draw it in faster, giving more current, which would draw it in even more: energy would be made from nothing. In fact you have to do work to push a magnet into a coil, and this work becomes electrical energy in the circuit (and heat in the resistance).","To use it: (1) say in which direction the original flux points, and whether it is increasing or decreasing. (2) The induced field must oppose the change: opposite to the original if increasing, along it if decreasing. (3) Use the right-hand rule to find the current direction that produces that field.","A neat way to see it with a magnet: a north pole moving towards a coil makes the near face of the coil a north pole (repelling it), and a north pole moving away makes the near face a south pole (attracting it back)."],"e":"A magnet's north pole moves towards a coil. The coil's near face becomes a north pole, so the current there is anticlockwise as seen from the magnet."},"12.4":{"s":"Magnetic flux through a surface is Φ = B·A = BA cos θ, the amount of field passing through it. Its unit is the weber. Flux can change if B changes, if the area changes, or if the angle between the field and the surface changes.","f":[{"x":"phi = B*A*cos(theta)","n":"Flux through a flat area A in a uniform field (phi = B·A); theta = angle between B and the normal to the area. In general phi = sum of B·da. Unit: weber (Wb).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.4, p.268"}],"w":["θ is measured from the normal (area vector), not from the surface: flux is largest at θ = 0."],"x":["The magnetic flux is the field times the area that is at right angles to it. For a uniform field, Φ = BA cosθ. When the field is not uniform, or the surface is curved, add up the contributions of small areas: Φ = ∫B · dA.","Think of the field lines as 'threads' going through the loop. The flux is proportional to the number of lines through it. If the loop is face-on to the field, θ = 0 and the flux is a maximum (BA). If the loop is edge-on, θ = 90° and the flux is zero.","The total flux through a closed surface is always zero (Gauss's law for magnetism), because magnetic field lines have no beginning or end: the same number go in as come out. This is a way of saying that magnetic monopoles do not exist.","For a coil of N turns, the flux linkage is NΦ. The emf depends on the rate of change of the flux linkage."],"e":"A coil of area 0.01 m² in a field of 0.5 T has a flux BA = 0.005 Wb when face-on, 0.0043 Wb when it is turned through 30°, and zero when edge-on."},"12.5":{"s":"A conductor of length l moving with speed v at right angles to a field B has an emf e = Blv across it. This is motional emf: the magnetic force pushes the charges along the rod. A rod rotating in a field also has an emf between its ends.","f":[{"x":"e = B*l*v","n":"Rod of length l moving at speed v at right angles to B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.5, p.269"},{"x":"e = (1/2)*B*omega*l^2","n":"Rod of length l turning about one end at angular speed omega in a field along the axis of turning.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.5, p.270"}],"w":["A rod moving along its own length, or parallel to B, has no motional emf."],"x":["Take a straight conducting rod of length L that moves with velocity v at right angles to a magnetic field B (and at right angles to its own length). The free electrons in the rod move with it, and feel a magnetic force qvB along the rod. They are pushed towards one end, which becomes negative, leaving the other end positive. This builds up an electric field that opposes the motion of more charge, until the forces balance.","The emf between the ends is the work done per unit charge by the magnetic force over the length: ε = BLv. If the rod slides on rails that form a closed circuit of resistance R, a current I = BLv/R flows. The direction of the current is given by Fleming's right-hand rule: with the thumb along the motion v, the first finger along B, the middle finger gives the current.","The flux approach gives the same result: the area of the circuit grows at a rate Lv, so dΦ/dt = BLv. By Lenz's law, the force on the current-carrying rod, F = BIL = B²L²v/R, opposes the motion, so you must push with this force, and the power you supply, Fv = B²L²v²/R, equals the electrical power I²R in the circuit. Energy is conserved.","For a rod of length L rotating about one end with angular speed ω in a perpendicular field, the average speed is ωL/2, so ε = ½BωL². An aeroplane flying horizontally has an emf between its wingtips because of the vertical part of the Earth's field."],"e":"A rod 0.4 m long moves at 5 m/s at right angles to a 0.5 T field: ε = 0.5 × 0.4 × 5 = 1.0 V. If the circuit has a resistance of 2 Ω, the current is 0.5 A."},"12.6":{"s":"A coil that does not move can still get an induced emf. It happens when the magnetic field through it changes. Examples: a magnet swinging past it, or a changing current in a nearby circuit. The emf is largest when the flux changes fastest.","w":["A stationary coil can still have an induced emf if the field through it changes with time.","The emf depends on the rate of change of flux, not on the flux itself."],"x":["In motional emf a conductor moves in a magnetic field. But an emf is also induced in a coil that is at rest, if the magnetic field passing through it changes. For example, a coil near another coil in which the current is switched on or off, or varies, will have an emf induced in it.","Here the magnetic force on the charges cannot be the cause, because the charges are not moving. Faraday's law still holds, so there must be an electric field. A changing magnetic field produces an induced electric field, which is different in character from the electrostatic field: its lines are closed loops, and it is non-conservative. The work done by it on a charge going once around a loop is the emf: ∮E · dl = −dΦ/dt.","This is one of Maxwell's four equations, and it is a deep fact: changing magnetic fields create electric fields. Together with the opposite effect (changing electric fields create magnetic fields, the displacement current), it leads to electromagnetic waves.","Applications: a transformer, an induction cooktop (a changing field induces currents in the base of the pan), a wireless phone charger, and the pickup coils in electric guitars and in credit card readers."],"e":"A coil of 200 turns and area 0.01 m² is in a field that grows from 0 to 0.5 T in 0.1 s. The induced emf is 200 × 0.01 × 0.5/0.1 = 10 V, though nothing moves."},"12.7":{"s":"An AC generator turns a coil in a magnetic field. The flux through it keeps changing, so an alternating emf e = e₀ sin ωt is produced, with e₀ = NBAω. Mechanical energy becomes electrical energy.","f":[{"x":"e = N*B*A*omega*sin(omega*t)","n":"AC generator: coil of N turns, area A, turning at angular speed omega.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.7, p.274"},{"x":"e_0 = N*B*A*omega","n":"Peak emf; reached when the coil's plane is parallel to B (flux zero, changing fastest).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.7, p.274"}],"w":["The emf is largest when the flux through the coil is zero, and zero when the flux is largest."],"x":["An AC generator (alternator) turns mechanical energy into electrical energy by electromagnetic induction. It has a coil (the armature) of N turns and area A that is made to rotate with angular speed ω in a uniform magnetic field B from a strong magnet. The ends of the coil are connected to slip rings that rub on carbon brushes, which lead the current out to the external circuit.","As the coil turns, the angle θ = ωt between B and the normal changes, so the flux Φ = NBA cosωt. The induced emf is ε = −dΦ/dt = NBAω sinωt = ε₀ sinωt, with peak value ε₀ = NBAω. It is a sinusoidal alternating emf of frequency f = ω/2π.","The emf is zero when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field (the flux is greatest, but not changing) and a maximum when the plane is parallel to the field (the flux is zero, but changing fastest). In 50 Hz mains, the emf changes direction 100 times a second.","In power stations, the coil is turned by a turbine, driven by steam (coal, nuclear), water (hydroelectric) or wind. A DC generator has a split-ring commutator in place of slip rings, which reverses the connection every half-turn so the output is in one direction, but pulsating."],"e":"A generator coil of 200 turns, area 0.05 m², turning at 50 revolutions per second in a field of 0.2 T gives a peak emf of 200 × 0.2 × 0.05 × 314 = 628 V."},"12.8":{"s":"In a running motor, the turning coil also acts as a generator and produces an emf that opposes the supply: the back emf. It limits the current once the motor is up to speed. In a generator, the induced current produces a back torque that opposes the rotation, so work is needed to keep it turning.","w":["A motor draws a large current at start because there is no back emf yet; that is why starters limit the current."],"x":["A DC motor has the same construction as a DC generator, but works in reverse: a current in the armature coil in a magnetic field gives a torque that makes it turn. But as soon as the coil turns, it is a coil rotating in a magnetic field, and so an emf is induced in it. By Lenz's law this emf opposes the supply voltage that is causing the rotation. It is the back emf, E.","The armature current is I = (V − E)/R, where V is the supply voltage and R the armature resistance. The back emf is proportional to the speed of the motor. When the motor starts from rest, E = 0, so the current is V/R, which is very large, and starters (resistors) are used with big motors. As the motor speeds up, E rises and the current falls. If the load is increased, the motor slows down, E falls, and more current flows, giving more torque. In this way a motor adjusts itself to the load.","The back torque is the opposing torque in a generator: when current is drawn from a generator, the current-carrying coil feels a torque, due to the magnetic field, that opposes its rotation. More electrical load means more back torque, and so more mechanical work is needed to keep the generator turning at the same speed. This is how energy is conserved.","The power input to the motor is VI = EI + I²R: the mechanical power is EI, and I²R is lost as heat."],"e":"A motor on 220 V with an armature resistance of 2 Ω draws 10 A while running, so the back emf is 220 − 20 = 200 V. At the instant of starting, the current would be 220/2 = 110 A."},"12.9":{"s":"Pulling a loop out of a magnetic field induces a current, and the field pushes back on that current. The work you do equals the electrical energy produced, which ends up as heat in the loop's resistance. Induction never gives free energy.","f":[{"x":"i = B*L*v/R","n":"Current in a loop of resistance R pulled out of a field at speed v; L = side cutting the field.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.9, p.276"},{"x":"F = B^2*L^2*v/R","n":"Force needed to pull the loop at constant speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.9, p.276"},{"x":"P = B^2*L^2*v^2/R","n":"Mechanical power you supply; equals the heat produced, i^2 R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.9, p.276"}],"w":["Induction does not create energy. The work done against the magnetic force is what becomes electrical energy.","If the induced current had the opposite direction, energy would not be conserved."],"x":["Electromagnetic induction is an example of conservation of energy. When a conductor is moved in a magnetic field, and a current is induced, the magnetic force on that current opposes the motion. To keep the conductor moving, an outside agent must do mechanical work, and this work is converted into electrical energy, and then into heat in the resistance of the circuit.","In the sliding rod on rails: the force needed to move the rod at a constant speed is F = BIL = B²L²v/R, the mechanical power delivered is Fv = B²L²v²/R, and the electrical power dissipated in the resistor is I²R = (BLv/R)²R = B²L²v²/R. The two are the same.","If the induced current did not oppose the motion, we would get more mechanical energy out than we put in, which would break the law of conservation of energy. So Lenz's law is the 'reason' behind the minus sign in Faraday's law.","This idea also explains why a generator is harder to turn when it supplies more current, and why a magnet falls slowly through a thick copper tube: the induced currents in the tube oppose the fall."],"e":"A rod of length 0.4 m moves at 5 m/s in a field of 0.5 T on rails with a resistance of 2 Ω. I = 0.5 A, the opposing force is 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.4 = 0.1 N, and the power is 0.1 × 5 = 0.5 W = I²R."},"12.10":{"s":"A changing magnetic flux through a solid metal plate induces circling currents in it, called eddy currents. They cause heating and braking. Uses: magnetic braking, induction furnaces, damping of meters. Laminated cores in transformers reduce unwanted eddy currents.","f":[{"x":"Induced circulating currents in a bulk conductor changing flux","n":"Eddy current. Cause heating; used in induction furnaces and brakes."}],"w":["Slots or thin insulated sheets (lamination) cut the paths of eddy currents and so reduce the heating."],"x":["When a solid piece of metal is in a changing magnetic field, or moves through a field, emfs are induced in it. Because the metal is a conductor in a big block, currents flow in closed loops in the bulk of the metal, like eddies in water. These are eddy currents (Foucault currents). By Lenz's law, they oppose the change that produces them.","They are often a nuisance: they heat the iron cores of transformers and motors, and waste energy. To reduce them, the cores are laminated: made of thin sheets, insulated from one another by varnish, so the eddy currents cannot flow in large loops and the resistance to them is large. Slots cut in a metal block do the same.","They are also useful. Induction furnaces heat metal by eddy currents with no contact (and in vacuum). Induction cooktops heat the pan. Magnetic braking in trains and roller coasters uses the drag of eddy currents on a metal plate moving through a field, with no wear. Electromagnetic damping is used in the pointer of a galvanometer and in the balance, to stop it from swinging. Metal detectors and eddy-current testing for cracks in aircraft parts also use them.","A demonstration: a strong magnet dropped through a thick copper or aluminium tube falls very slowly, since the eddy currents in the tube make a field that opposes its fall."],"e":"In a train with magnetic brakes, a metal rail passing between the poles of electromagnets feels a drag that is proportional to the speed, so it slows the train smoothly with no friction."},"12.11":{"s":"When the current in a coil changes, its own flux changes and induces an emf that opposes the change: self-induction. e = −L di/dt, where L is the self-inductance in henry. For a long solenoid L = μ₀n²Al, so more turns and a larger area give more inductance.","f":[{"x":"N*phi = L*i","n":"Flux linkage of a coil carrying current i; L = self-inductance in henry.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.278"},{"x":"e = -L*di/dt","n":"Self-induced emf.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.278"},{"x":"L = mu_0*n^2*A*l","n":"Long solenoid; n = turns per unit length, A = cross-section, l = length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.279"},{"x":"L = L_1 + L_2 + ...","n":"Inductors in series (no mutual coupling); in parallel 1/L = 1/L_1 + 1/L_2 + ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.280"}],"w":["Inductance depends only on the coil's shape, turns and core, not on the current."],"x":["When the current in a coil changes, the flux through the coil itself changes, and so an emf is induced in it. This is self-induction. By Lenz's law, the self-induced emf opposes the change of current: it slows the rise of a growing current and the fall of a decreasing one.","The flux linked with the coil is proportional to the current: NΦ = LI. The constant L is the self-inductance (inductance) of the coil, in henry (H = Wb/A = V s/A). Then the induced emf is ε = −L dI/dt. L plays the part of inertia for an electric current, just as mass does for motion.","L depends only on the geometry of the coil and the core material, not on the current. For a long solenoid with N turns, length l and area A, L = μ₀N²A/l = μ₀n²Al. An iron core multiplies L by the relative permeability (hundreds or thousands). More turns, a larger area, or a core of iron give a larger L; the dependence on N is as N².","Examples: a spark when a switch is opened in a circuit with a large coil, the high voltage in the ignition coil of a petrol engine, the choke (inductor) in a tube light, and the delay in the growth of current in an LR circuit, with a time constant τ = L/R."],"e":"The current in a coil of 0.5 H changes by 2 A in 0.1 s: the induced emf is 0.5 × 2/0.1 = 10 V."},"12.12":{"s":"Building up a current in an inductor needs work against the back emf. That work is stored in the magnetic field: U = ½ L i². It is given back when the current falls.","f":[{"x":"U = (1/2)*L*i^2","n":"Energy stored in an inductor carrying current i.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.12, p.280"}],"w":["The energy is stored in the field, not in the wire. It is half L i squared.","The energy is given back when the current falls, so no net energy is lost in an ideal inductor."],"x":["When a current is set up in a coil, the self-induced emf opposes its growth, so the source must do work against it. This work is stored as energy in the magnetic field of the coil, and is given back when the current falls.","The power needed at a moment is P = εI = LI dI/dt, so the work done in raising the current from 0 to I is the integral of LI dI = ½LI². So the energy stored in an inductor is U = ½LI². It is the magnetic counterpart of the energy ½CV² in a capacitor and of the kinetic energy ½mv².","When the circuit is broken, the field collapses, and this energy is released quickly, often as a spark across the switch. That is why a large coil needs a protective diode across it.","Notice that the energy depends on the square of the current: if the current is doubled, the energy is four times as large."],"e":"A coil of 2 H carrying 3 A stores ½ × 2 × 9 = 9 J. The same coil carrying 6 A stores 36 J."},"12.13":{"s":"The energy stored in a magnetic field per unit volume (energy density) is B²/(2μ₀). It depends only on the field, just as electric field energy depends on E.","f":[{"x":"u_B = B^2/(2*mu_0)","n":"Magnetic energy per unit volume.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.13, p.281"}],"w":["Energy density is energy per unit volume. It has no coil or current in it, only the field.","Do not mix it up with the total energy stored, which also depends on the volume."],"x":["The energy stored in an inductor is in the magnetic field, and not in the wire. To see this, take a long solenoid with a field B = μ₀nI inside, a volume Al, and inductance L = μ₀n²Al. The energy is U = ½LI² = ½μ₀n²AlI².","Dividing by the volume Al, the energy density is u = ½μ₀n²I² = B²/(2μ₀). This is true of any magnetic field in vacuum, in general. In a material of permeability μ, u = B²/(2μ).","Compare the energy density of an electric field, u = ½ε₀E². Both are proportional to the square of the field, and both show that fields are real things that carry energy through space. Electromagnetic waves, in which E and B are both present, carry energy of density ½ε₀E² + B²/2μ₀, equally shared between the electric and magnetic parts.","The energy density of a 1 T field is (1)²/(2 × 4π × 10⁻⁷) = 4 × 10⁵ J/m³, which is large. That is why strong magnets such as MRI scanners have a lot of stored energy."],"e":"In a solenoid with B = 0.01 T, the energy per unit volume is (0.01)²/(2 × 4π × 10⁻⁷) = 39.8 J/m³."},"12.14":{"s":"When the current in one coil changes, it changes the flux through a nearby coil and induces an emf there: mutual induction. e₂ = −M di₁/dt, where M is the mutual inductance. M is larger when the coils are close, share an iron core, and have more turns.","f":[{"x":"e_2 = -M*dI_1/dt","n":"Emf in coil 2 from a changing current in coil 1; M_12 = M_21 = M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.14, p.282"},{"x":"M = K*sqrt(L_1*L_2)","n":"K = coefficient of coupling (at most 1; 1 for perfect coupling).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.14, p.283"}],"w":["Coils with axes at right angles have the least coupling; on one iron core K is close to 1."],"x":["Two coils are placed close together. A changing current in coil 1 produces a changing flux, part of which goes through coil 2, and induces an emf in it. This is mutual induction. The flux through coil 2 is proportional to the current in coil 1: N₂Φ₂ = MI₁. The constant M is the mutual inductance, in henry. The induced emf in coil 2 is ε₂ = −M dI₁/dt.","The mutual inductance is the same in both directions (M₁₂ = M₂₁). It depends on the number of turns of each coil, their size and shape, how close they are and how they are oriented, and the medium between them (an iron core increases M a lot). It does not depend on the currents. The coupling is best when the coils are wound on the same iron core, and weakest when they are far apart or at right angles.","For two coaxial solenoids, one inside the other, with a common length l, area A and turns N₁ and N₂, M = μ₀N₁N₂A/l. The maximum possible M for two coils is √(L₁L₂), when all the flux of one passes through the other.","Applications: the transformer, the induction coil that produces high voltage from a low-voltage battery, wireless charging, and sensors."],"e":"If the current in one coil changes at 3 A/s and an emf of 6 V is induced in a nearby coil, M = 6/3 = 2 H."},"12.15":{"s":"A transformer changes AC voltage using mutual induction between two coils on an iron core. Vs/Vp = Ns/Np. A step-up transformer has more turns in the secondary; a step-down transformer has fewer. Ideally power in equals power out, so current changes the opposite way. It does not work with steady DC.","f":[{"x":"e_s/e_p = N_s/N_p = i_p/i_s","n":"Ideal transformer: voltage goes with turns, current the opposite way (power in = power out).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.15, p.285"}],"w":["Step-up raises voltage but lowers current; it does not make extra power."],"x":["A transformer changes an alternating voltage from one value to another. It has two coils, the primary (with N_p turns) and the secondary (N_s turns), wound on a common soft iron core, which guides the flux so that nearly all of it passes through both coils. It works by mutual induction. An alternating current in the primary produces an alternating flux in the core, which induces an emf in the secondary.","For an ideal transformer (no losses), the same flux passes through each turn of each coil, so V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. If N_s is greater than N_p, it is a step-up transformer (the voltage rises). If N_s is less than N_p, it is a step-down transformer. The power is the same on both sides, V_pI_p = V_sI_s, so I_p/I_s = N_s/N_p = V_s/V_p: a higher voltage means a lower current.","Real transformers lose some energy: copper losses (I²R heating in the windings), iron losses (eddy currents and hysteresis in the core), flux leakage (not all of the flux links the secondary), and a small amount by humming (magnetostriction). Laminated cores of silicon steel, thick wire and good winding keep the efficiency above 95%. A transformer does not work on direct current, since a steady current gives a steady flux and no induced emf.","Use: electricity is sent over long distances at very high voltage (132 kV to 765 kV), so that the current is small, and the I²R loss in the lines is small. It is stepped down at substations, and again to 230 V for homes. Phone chargers, adaptors, and welding machines all use transformers."],"e":"A transformer with 500 primary and 50 secondary turns on a 220 V supply gives 22 V. If it delivers 10 A, the primary draws 22 × 10/220 = 1 A."}};
